WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.800
Seven years ago, back in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.640
one major development in AI research&nbsp;
was automated image captioning.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.880
Machine learning algorithms could&nbsp;
already label objects in images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.160
and now they learned to put those labels&nbsp;
into natural language descriptions.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.040
And it made one group of researchers curious.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.120
What if you flipped that process around?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.400
We could do image to text.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.240
Why not try doing text to&nbsp;
images and see how it works?

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.240
It was a more difficult task.They didn’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.120
to retrieve existing images&nbsp;
the way google search does.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.448
They wanted to generate entirely novel scenes 
that didn’t happen in the real world.

00:00:35.448 --> 00:00:39.680
So they asked their computer model for 
something it would have never seen before.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.840
Like all the school buses you've seen are yellow.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.840
But if you write “the red or green school bus”&nbsp;
would it actually try to generate something green?

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:48.480
And it did that.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.160
It was a 32 by 32 tiny image.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.320
And then all you could see is like a&nbsp;
blob of something on top of something.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.920
They tried some other prompts like “A herd&nbsp;
of elephants flying in the blue skies”.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.080
“A vintage photo of a cat.”

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.520
“A toilet seat sits open in the grass field.”

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.600
And “a bowl of bananas is on the table.”

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.600
Maybe not something to hang on your wall&nbsp;
but the 2016 paper from those researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.560
showed the potential for what might&nbsp;
become possible in the future.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.200
And uh... the future has arrived.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.720
It is almost impossible to overstate how far&nbsp;
the technology has come in just one year.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:31.600
By leaps and bounds.
Leaps and bounds.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.960
Yeah, it's been quite dramatic.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.640
I don’t know anyone who&nbsp;
hasn’t immediately been like

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.880
“What is this? What is happening here?”

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.840
Could I say like watching waves crashing?

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.160
Party hat guy.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.120
Seafoam dreams.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.560
A coral reef.
Cubism.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:55.200
Caterpillar.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.240
A dancing taco.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.400
My prompt is Salvador Dali painting&nbsp;
the skyline of New York City.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.000
You may be thinking, wait&nbsp;
AI-generated images aren’t new.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.080
You probably heard about this generated portrait&nbsp;
going for over $400,000 at auction back in 2018.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.920
Or this installation of morphing portraits,&nbsp;
which Sotheby’s sold the following year.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.040
It was created by Mario Klingemann, who&nbsp;
explained to me that that type of AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:27.040
art required him to collect a specific dataset of&nbsp;
images and train his own model to mimic that data.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.360
Let's say, Oh, I want to create landscapes,&nbsp;
so I collect a lot of landscape images.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.320
I want to create portraits,&nbsp;
I trained on portraits.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.800
But then the portrait model would not&nbsp;
really be able to create landscapes.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.760
Same with those hyper realistic&nbsp;
fake faces that have been plaguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.480
linkedin and facebook – those come from a&nbsp;
model that only knows how to make faces.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.240
Generating a scene from any combination of words&nbsp;
requires a different, newer, bigger approach.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.880
Now we kind of have these huge&nbsp;
models, which are so huge that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.640
somebody like me actually cannot train&nbsp;
them anymore on their own computer.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.360
But once they are there, they are&nbsp;
really kind of— they contain everything.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.360
I mean, to a certain extent.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.720
What this means is that we can now&nbsp;
create images without having to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.160
execute them with paint or&nbsp;
cameras or pen tools or code.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.840
The input is just a simple line of text.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.120
I'll get to how this tech works later in the video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.280
but to understand how we got here,&nbsp;
we have to rewind to January 2021

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.000
When a major AI company called Open AI announced&nbsp;
DALL-E – which they named after these guys.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.560
They said it could create images from text&nbsp;
captions for a wide range of concepts.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.000
They recently announced DALLE-2, which promises&nbsp;
more realistic results and seamless editing.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.040
But they haven’t released&nbsp;
either version to the public.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.520
So over the past year, a community of&nbsp;
independent, open-source developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.080
built text-to-image generators out of other&nbsp;
pre-trained models that they did have access to.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.720
And you can play with those online for free.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.680
Some of those developers are now working&nbsp;
for a company called Midjourney,&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.800
which created a Discord community with bots that&nbsp;
turn your text into images in less than a minute.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.960
Having basically no barrier to entry to&nbsp;
this has made it like a whole new ballgame.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.640
I've been up until like two&nbsp;
or three in the morning.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.480
Just really trying to change things, piece things together.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.040
I've done about 7,000 images. It’s ridiculous.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.840
MidJourney currently has a wait-list for&nbsp;
subscriptions, but we got a chance to try it out.

00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:31.536
"Go ahead and take a look."

00:04:31.536 --> 00:04:34.880
“Oh wow. That is so cool”

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.320
“It has some work to do. I feel like it can&nbsp;
be — it’s not dancing and it could be better.”

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.560
The craft of communicating&nbsp;
with these deep learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.040
models has been dubbed “prompt engineering”.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.600
What I love about prompting&nbsp;
for me, it's kind of really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.040
that has something like magic where you have to&nbsp;
know the right words for that, for the spell.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.880
You realize that you can refine&nbsp;
the way you talk to the machine.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.040
It becomes a kind of a dialog.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.480
You can say like “octane render blender 3D”.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.840
Made with Unreal Engine...

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.640
...certain types of film lenses and cameras...

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.880
...1950s, 1960s...

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.400
...dates are really good.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.920
...lino cut or wood cut...

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.520
Coming up with funny pairings, like a Faberge Egg McMuffin.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.640
A monochromatic infographic poster about&nbsp;
typography depicting Chinese characters.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.360
Some of the most striking images&nbsp;
can come from prompting the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.000
to synthesize a long list of concepts.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.680
It's kind of like it's having a very strange&nbsp;
collaborator to bounce ideas off of and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.840
unpredictable ideas back.

00:05:42.177 --> 00:05:44.183
I love that!

00:05:44.183 --> 00:05:47.359
My prompt was "chasing seafoam dreams,"

00:05:47.359 --> 00:05:51.441
which is a lyric from the Ted Leo and the Pharmacists' song "Biomusicology."

00:05:51.441 --> 00:05:55.588
Can I use this as the album cover for my first album? "Absolutely."

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.311
Alright.

00:05:58.576 --> 00:06:01.440
For an image generator to be able to&nbsp;
respond to so many different prompts,&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.840
it needs a massive, diverse training dataset.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.320
Like hundreds of millions of images scraped from&nbsp;
the internet, along with their text descriptions.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.880
Those captions come from things like the alt text&nbsp;
that website owners upload with their images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.120
for accessibility and for search engines.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.320
So that’s how the engineers&nbsp;
get these giant datasets.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:20.560
But then what do the models actually do with them?

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.000
We might assume that when&nbsp;
we give them a text prompt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.440
like “a banana inside a snow globe from 1960."

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.960
They search through the training data&nbsp;
to find related images and then copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.240
over some of those pixels. But&nbsp;
that’s not what’s happening.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.240
The new generated image doesn’t&nbsp;
come from the training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.360
it comes from the “latent space”&nbsp;
of the deep learning model.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.120
That’ll make sense in a minute, first&nbsp;
let’s look at how the model learns.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.480
If I gave you these images and told you to match&nbsp;
them to these captions, you’d have no problem.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.520
But what about now, this is&nbsp;
what images look like to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.320
machine just pixel values for red green and blue.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.080
You’d just have to make a guess, and&nbsp;
that’s what the computer does too at first.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.240
But then you could go through&nbsp;
thousands of rounds of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.160
and never figure out how to get better at it.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.600
Whereas a computer can eventually figure out a&nbsp;
method that works- that’s what deep learning does.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:13.920
In order to understand that this arrangement&nbsp;
of pixels is a banana, and this arrangement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.120
of pixels is a balloon, it looks for metrics that&nbsp;
help separate these images in mathematical space.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.040
So how about color? If we measure&nbsp;
the amount of yellow in the image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.240
that would put the banana over here and the&nbsp;
balloon over here in this one-dimensional space.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.160
But then what if we run into this:

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.560
Now our yellowness metric isn’t very&nbsp;
good at separating bananas from balloons.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.160
We need a different variable.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.400
Let’s add an axis for roundness.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:44.160
Now we’ve got a two dimensional space with the&nbsp;
round balloons up here and the banana down here.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.760
But if we look at more data we may come&nbsp;
across a banana that’s pretty round,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.280
and a balloon that isn’t.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.240
So maybe there’s some way to measure shininess.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.240
Balloons usually have a shiny spot.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.440
Now we have a three dimensional space.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.440
And ideally, when we get a new image we&nbsp;
can measure those 3 variables and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.720
whether it falls in the banana region&nbsp;
or the balloon region of the space.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.040
But what if we want our model to recognize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.080
not just bananas and balloons,&nbsp;
but…all these other things.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.880
Yellowness, roundness, and shininess don’t&nbsp;
capture what’s distinct about these objects.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.560
That’s what deep learning algorithms do&nbsp;
as they go through all the training data.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.120
They find variables that help improve their&nbsp;
performance on the task and in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:30.560
they build out a mathematical space&nbsp;
with way more than 3 dimensions.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:37.520
We are incapable of picturing multidimensional&nbsp;
space, but midjourney's model offered this and I like it.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.120
So we’ll say this represents the latent space of&nbsp;
the model. And It has more than 500 dimensions.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.080
Those 500 axes represent variables that&nbsp;
humans wouldn’t even recognize or have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:51.840
names for but the result is that&nbsp;
the space has meaningful clusters:

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.800
A region that captures the essence of banana-ness.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.200
A region that represents the textures&nbsp;
and colors of photos from the 1960s.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.680
An area for snow and an area for globes&nbsp;
and snowglobes somewhere in between.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.160
Any point in this space can be thought&nbsp;
of as the recipe for a possible image.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:16.560
The text prompt is what navigates us to that&nbsp;
location. But then there’s one more step.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.160
Translating a point in that mathematical&nbsp;
space into an actual image involves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.480
generative process called diffusion.&nbsp;
It starts with just noise and then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.520
over a series of iterations, arranges pixels&nbsp;
into a composition that makes sense to humans.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:34.880
Because of some randomness in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.000
it will never return exactly the&nbsp;
same image for the same prompt.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.520
And if you enter the prompt into a&nbsp;
different model designed by different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:44.880
people and trained on different&nbsp;
data, you’ll get a different result.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.840
Because you’re in a different latent space.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:06.880
No way. That is so cool. What the heck? The brush&nbsp;
strokes, the color palette. That’s fascinating.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:12.119
I wish I could like — I mean he’s dead,&nbsp;
but go up to him and be like, "Look what I have!"

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.120
Oh that’s pretty cool. Probably the&nbsp;
only Dali that I could afford anyways.”

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.440
The ability of deep learning to extract&nbsp;
patterns from data means that you can copy an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:31.840
artist’s style without copying their images,&nbsp;
just by putting their name in the prompt.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.720
James Gurney is an American illustrator who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:38.080
became a popular reference for&nbsp;
users of text to image models.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:43.120
I asked him what kind of norms he would like&nbsp;
to see as prompting becomes widespread.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.760
I think it's only fair to&nbsp;
people looking at this work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:50.000
that they should know what the prompt&nbsp;
was and also what software was used.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.320
Also I think the artists should be allowed&nbsp;
to opt in or opt out of having their work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:59.760
that they worked so hard on by hand be used&nbsp;
as a dataset for creating this other artwork.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.520
James Gurney, I think he was a&nbsp;
great example of being someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.200
who was open to it, started&nbsp;
talking with the artists.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:13.280
But I also heard of other artists&nbsp;
who got actually extremely upset.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:16.480
The copyright questions regarding&nbsp;
the images that go into training the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.800
models and the images that come out&nbsp;
of them…are completely unresolved.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.360
And those aren’t the only questions&nbsp;
that this technology will provoke.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.160
The latent space of these models contains some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:30.400
dark corners that get scarier as&nbsp;
outputs become photorealistic.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.760
It also holds an untold number&nbsp;
of associations that we wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.640
teach our children but that&nbsp;
it learned from the internet.

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:40.400
If you ask an image of the CEO,&nbsp;
it's like an old white guy.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.120
If you ask for images of&nbsp;
nurses, they're all like women.

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:47.760
We don’t know exactly what’s in the&nbsp;
datasets used by OpenAI or Midjourney.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:51.120
But we know the internet is biased toward&nbsp;
the English language and western concepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.760
with whole cultures not represented at all.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.280
In one open-sourced dataset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.760
the word “asian” is represented first&nbsp;
and foremost by an avalanche of porn.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:07.600
It really is just sort of an infinitely complex&nbsp;
mirror held up to our society and what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.840
deemed worthy enough to, you know, put&nbsp;
on the internet in the first place and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.320
how we think about what we do put up.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.840
But what makes this technology so&nbsp;
unique is that it enables any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:23.120
us to direct the machine to&nbsp;
imagine what we want it to see.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:29.040
Party hat guy, space invader, caterpillar, and a ramen bowl. 

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:35.120
Prompting removes the obstacles between ideas&nbsp;
and images, and eventually videos, animations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:36.880
and whole virtual worlds.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:42.560
We are on a voyage here, that&nbsp;
is it's a bigger deal than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:46.240
than just like one decade or the&nbsp;
immediate technical consequences.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:50.960
It's a change in the way humans imagine,&nbsp;
communicate, work with their own culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:56.080
And that will have long range,&nbsp;
good and bad consequences that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.080 --> 00:13:01.040
we are just by definition, not going to&nbsp;
be capable of completely anticipating.

00:13:05.997 --> 00:13:09.749
Over the course of researching this video 
I spoke to a bunch of creative people

00:13:09.749 --> 00:13:11.328
who have played with these tools.

00:13:11.328 --> 00:13:16.834
And I asked them what they think this all means for people who make a living making images.

00:13:16.834 --> 00:13:22.081
The human artists and illustrators and designers and stock photographers out there.

00:13:22.081 --> 00:13:24.775
And they had a lot of interesting things to say. 

00:13:24.775 --> 00:13:27.345
So I've compiled them into a bonus video.

00:13:27.345 --> 00:13:31.996
Please check it out and add your own thoughts in the comments. Thank you for watching.

